  "text_md": "<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nBIORETENTION SITING DESIGN &amp; CONSTRUCTION\n\n## INFEASIBILITY CRITERIA: Infiltration Setbacks\n\nFeature\n\nSetback\n\nNative Growth Protection Easement\n\n2 20 feet\n\nTop of slopes &gt;20% and over 10 feet of relief\n\n2 50 feet\n\nUnderground storage tanks\n\n10-100 feet\n\nWellheads basements foundations, utilities, slopes contaminated areas, and property lines\n\nConsult local jurisdiction guidelines\n\nSetbacks (source: SWMMWW Infeasibility Criteria)\n\nStatewide LID Training Program\n\nBIORETENTION\n\nECOLOGY\n\nBIORETENTION SITING DESIGN &amp; CONSTRUCTION\n\n## SITING CONSIDERATIONS\n\n- Understand fate of infiltrated water\n\n- Intent is to infiltrate to native underlying soil\n\n- Arterial ROW with dense underground infrastructure (preferential pathway &gt; utility trenches)\n\n- Potential for excessive shallow interflow emerging at slopes, development cuts, or in basements\n\n- engineering controls Use\n\n- Ex. trench water stops to prevent re-infiltration to pipes\n\n- Ex. liners to protect adjacent infrastructure\n\nStatewide LID Training Program\n\nBIORETENTION\n\nECOLOGY\n\nINTERMEDIATE lID DESIGN\n\n## BIORETENTION SITING DESIGN &amp; CONSTRUCTION SITING CONSIDERATIONS\n\n- Native soil and vegetation preservation\n\n- Site Slopes\n\n- Cross &amp; Longitudinal Slopes\n\n- Positive Drainage from drainage area to BR to overflow\n\n- Setbacks (e.8., utilities &amp; other infrastructure, wetland and streams)\n\n2012 LID Technical Guidance Manual for Puget Sound\n\n- Public acceptance/participation (retrofits)\n\n- Transportation /pedestrian safety\n\nStatewide LID Training Program\n\nBIORETENTION\n\nECOLOGY\n\nINTERMEDIATE lID DESIGN",
